Using the balance method, the fraction at time \(t\) is found by first writing a <cite id="2a0c378e-0">material balance as an accounting for material</cite> with the general form <cite id="2a0c378e-0">Input + generation = output + consumption</cite>, or equivalently including accumulation as <cite id="2a0c378e-2">Input − Output + Generation − Consumption = Accumulation</cite>.  
When <cite id="2b688bf8-0">process variables change over time</cite>, you solve this balance for the amount of the component present as a function of time, \(N(t)\). The fraction is then obtained by dividing that time-dependent amount by the corresponding total amount, e.g. \(f(t)=N(t)/N_{\text{total}}(t)\). In short: <cite id="2a0c378e-2">use the balance to compute accumulation over time</cite>, then convert the resulting amount into a fraction by normalization.

\boxed{\text{Determine the component amount from the time-dependent balance (accumulation = input - output + generation - consumption), then compute the fraction as }f(t)=\frac{N(t)}{N_{\text{total}}(t)}.}
